result:
The design: tenant comes from the credential (never the URL), resources are collections → documents → immutable revisions, lists use opaque cursors, the API version is a path major (`/v1`), and errors are RFC 9457 `application/problem+json`.

```
GET|POST          /v1/collections
GET|PATCH|DELETE  /v1/collections/{collection_id}
GET|POST          /v1/collections/{collection_id}/documents
GET|PUT|PATCH|DELETE /v1/documents/{document_id}
GET               /v1/documents/{document_id}/revisions
GET               /v1/documents/{document_id}/revisions/{rev}
POST              /v1/documents/{document_id}/restore      {"rev": 7}
GET|POST          /v1/api-keys          DELETE /v1/api-keys/{key_id}
GET|POST          /v1/members           PATCH|DELETE /v1/members/{user_id}
```

**Resources.** Documents are created under a collection but addressed flat by a globally unique, opaque, prefixed ID (`doc_01J...`), so a client holding an ID needs no parent lookup and moving a document doesn't change its URL. A document is an envelope (`id`, `collection_id`, `rev`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `metadata`) around a `content` JSON body. Every write creates a new immutable revision, and restore is a new write rather than a rewind, so history stays append-only. Deletes are soft (`deleted_at`, purged after a retention window).

**Concurrency.** `GET` returns an `ETag` derived from `rev`. `PUT`, `PATCH` and `DELETE` require `If-Match`, returning 428 when it is missing and 412 when it is stale, so lost updates are impossible by default. `POST` accepts an `Idempotency-Key` so retries after a timeout don't create duplicates.

**Auth.** There are two credential types: OIDC-issued short-lived JWTs for users and hashed, revocable API keys for services. Both resolve server-side to the same triple of tenant, principal and scopes (`documents:read`, `documents:write`, `collections:admin`, `tenant:admin`). Each credential is bound to exactly one tenant, and every query is filtered by that tenant ID in the data layer. Keeping the tenant out of the path means editing a URL can never cross tenants, and a forgotten check fails closed. A user in several tenants gets one token per tenant. Requests for another tenant's resource return 404, not 403, so IDs can't be probed for existence.

**Pagination.** List endpoints take `?limit=` (default 50, max 200) and `?cursor=`, and return `{"data": [...], "next_cursor": "...", "has_more": true}`. The cursor is an opaque, signed encoding of the sort key, the ID and the filters in use. Unlike offsets, this stays correct under concurrent writes and costs the same at any depth. There is no total count, because it forces a full scan per tenant. Changing filters or sort mid-walk returns 400.

**Versioning.** The path carries only the major version. Additive changes (new fields, endpoints, enum values) ship inside `/v1`, and clients must ignore unknown fields. Breaking changes mean `/v2`, with the old major emitting `Deprecation` and `Sunset` headers for at least 12 months. I chose the path over a header because it is visible in logs, trivially routable and cacheable, and hard to get wrong.

**Errors.** Every non-2xx response has the same shape:

```json
{
  "type": "https://api.example.com/errors/validation-failed",
  "title": "Validation failed",
  "status": 422,
  "code": "validation_failed",
  "detail": "2 fields are invalid.",
  "request_id": "req_01J9...",
  "errors": [
    {"field": "metadata.title", "code": "too_long", "message": "Maximum 256 characters."}
  ]
}
```

Clients branch on `code`, which is stable and documented, and never on `title` or `detail`, which are for humans. `request_id` is also sent as a header for support correlation. The statuses used are 400 (malformed), 401, 403 (lacks scope within its own tenant), 404, 409 (conflict), 412, 422, 428, and 429 with `Retry-After`. Rate limits are counted per tenant, so one noisy tenant can't starve the others.

I left out search, bulk and batch writes, webhooks or change feeds, and per-document ACLs. Add per-collection grants first if tenant-level roles prove too coarse.
